The lock nut does the work of mounting. Tightening it drives the bearing along the sleeve taper, and how far it is tightened is what sets the bearing internal clearance once mounted. That makes it a functional component, not simply a fastener.

Slots machined into the outer face take a hook spanner or impact spanner. Once the bearing is correctly seated, a lock washer is bent into one of those slots to stop the nut backing off under vibration.

Kaybee International stocks lock nuts across the sizes matching our sleeve and housing ranges, and supplies non-standard sizes to requirement.
